n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186124992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186124992 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186124992 1071 "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}, C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}, [x]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of $R^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}$." 13194139534836 17592186045417 false] [17592186124992 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186125034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122804 13194139534836 17592186045417 false] [17592186125034 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186125034 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 false] [17592186351443 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186351443 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 false] [17592186351443 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\