61 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440449 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440461 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440450 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440461 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440461 1077 "selection" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440461 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440450 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440461 1079 #uuid "db389e57-4899-5138-b87d-2318a36bc5bc" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440453 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440462 1069 "q-82699" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440462 1071 "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440462 1072 #uuid "38a4fba0-c09e-5c16-89d3-da5b6bc68d95" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440462 1074 "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440457 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440463 1069 "q-113707" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440463 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440463 1072 #uuid "4f73a710-afa9-5a82-9ac8-46435d104af8" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440463 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440460 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440464 1069 "q-82706" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440464 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440464 1072 #uuid "50a9b032-ab11-5594-a8b4-f98e14bf6ea6" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440464 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 7-12\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440461 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440465 1069 "q-113593" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440465 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440465 1072 #uuid "7107964a-37f7-5d19-a3ab-82f544f38d24" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440465 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440454 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440466 1069 "q-113539" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440466 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440466 1072 #uuid "769b83ac-7384-56eb-8f7a-6aa51afc8a2a" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440466 1074 "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440459 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440467 1069 "q-113694" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440467 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440467 1072 #uuid "7ff42b5f-a341-579b-8b7f-9bd10f63610f" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440467 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440455 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440468 1069 "q-82620" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440468 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440468 1072 #uuid "92fb3400-1e9e-5f15-b0ad-6ea07e725536" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440468 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 13 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440456 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440469 1069 "q-82601" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440469 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440469 1072 #uuid "9bc30c67-14a3-514e-87f9-9c3583feaf85" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440469 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440458 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440470 1069 "q-113599" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440470 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440470 1072 #uuid "b6330ac1-ad9e-564b-a472-75f385cd01df" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true] [17592186440470 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534836 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-05a2a04d-6fe7-5a0d-8718-80558dc272b6" 17592186440398 "answer-07df0399-5b7b-513e-bfe4-682fc2093392" 17592186440399 "answer-10cf25e0-86f0-5251-8e30-bdb8e6de1aee" 17592186440400 "answer-1716bdc6-24bd-510f-89d5-ab35a3bdb21f" 17592186440401 "answer-17b92d00-0a4a-59f6-a147-1be3ac44c4da" 17592186440402 "answer-182873b5-14af-5685-85b8-f194b83b4644" 17592186440403 "answer-18dc48a1-5985-578f-8b5e-9016d734956d" 17592186440404 "answer-1b76988c-1557-52a5-91a3-c4a65053602e" 17592186440405 "answer-1bceb481-65f5-5e67-97b5-3ca99d15ac64" 17592186440406 "answer-1f5c1c5a-e4ea-5d65-95cb-9752b676bf64" 17592186440407 "answer-27f96cc5-65da-5d59-b093-95e1e75c4935" 17592186440408 "answer-2c1b735c-b2d7-53c7-85cc-47c1e2305f6d" 17592186440409 "answer-3371709f-eb7b-58f8-93cb-bb7d689a3fbb" 17592186440410 "answer-3aa4bb15-cd67-5f6e-b5ce-1a199a6dd8a8" 17592186440411 "answer-3c50c8f3-2ffa-52d6-b0c4-ad1154deb379" 17592186440412 "answer-3fffbe35-a610-576c-8afc-bc0238c80168" 17592186440413 "answer-45687a63-f60a-5bfb-a261-9e94c6a4c076" 17592186440414 "answer-4616c187-092f-502c-9325-a8e520cc53fb" 17592186440415 "answer-4741e426-3e99-5468-9275-81e55bc048d9" 17592186440416 "answer-4b07ccd2-15b5-5fe4-9051-5d908e60ebd4" 17592186440417 "answer-4bf44605-44d3-5b61-899c-88515785b624" 17592186440418 "answer-507e0790-a1c9-534c-b2c6-1884ab0a08e2" 17592186440419 "answer-50b3b88a-a623-50e5-b968-d9227a27e597" 17592186440420 "answer-50b8fde9-b1bf-55f1-85e4-62be49bc498f" 17592186440421 "answer-5583d439-4b26-52b3-9794-1635eb018312" 17592186440422 "answer-5ed1a788-27da-548c-9bec-4712cdd0d589" 17592186440423 "answer-66289914-3007-51f5-9e3c-d8f37baa6eeb" 17592186440424 "answer-6e3b207a-4928-510d-a62a-dee0e13ac5c8" 17